{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q003_actin_like_atpase_divergence","answer":"SCOPe places these catalytic domains in distinct families—Actin/HSP70, hexokinase, and glycerol kinase—within the Actin-like ATPase domain superfamily (c.55.1). This superfamily belongs to the ribonuclease H-like motif fold (c.55), in the α/β protein class.

Their shared superfamily supports remote common ancestry and conservation of a nucleotide-binding catalytic core despite substantial functional divergence. Actin couples ATP turnover to cytoskeletal assembly and dynamics; HSP70 couples it to chaperone activity; hexokinase transfers phosphate to hexoses; glycerol kinase transfers phosphate to glycerol. Thus, the superfamily name does not imply that every member functions primarily as an ATP-hydrolyzing enzyme.

This relationship supports comparisons of the conserved structural core and can guide remote-homology modeling. It does not justify transferring substrate specificity, cellular role, interaction partners, or detailed catalytic mechanisms between families. Such transfers require sequence and active-site comparisons, ligand-bound structures, and biochemical evidence. A cross-family structural template may help model the core but is less reliable for divergent insertions and substrate-binding regions. The broader shared fold alone carries weaker evolutionary evidence and does not establish homology to every other protein with that fold.","evidence_used":["pretrained model knowledge"]}]}
